                <span class="lesson-number">Lesson 1</span>
                <h1>Introduction to Blazor</h1>
                <div class="lesson-meta"><span class="lesson-duration"><svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"/><polyline points="12 6 12 12 16 14"/></svg>25 min</span><span class="lesson-level intermediate">Intermediate</span></div>
            </header>
            <div class="lesson-body">
                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Welcome to Blazor!</h2>
                    <p>Blazor is a framework for building interactive web UIs using C# instead of JavaScript. You can create rich, interactive web applications while leveraging your existing .NET skills and sharing code between client and server.</p>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>What is Blazor?</h2>
                    <p>Blazor lets you build interactive web UIs using C# and .NET. The name "Blazor" comes from "Browser" + "Razor" (the .NET view engine).</p>
                    <div class="info-box">
                        <div class="info-box-header"><svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"/><line x1="12" y1="16" x2="12" y2="12"/><line x1="12" y1="8" x2="12.01" y2="8"/></svg><span>Key Benefits</span></div>
                        <ul>
                            <li>Write C# instead of JavaScript</li>
                            <li>Share code between client and server</li>
                            <li>Use the .NET ecosystem and NuGet packages</li>
                            <li>Full debugging support in Visual Studio/VS Code</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Blazor Hosting Models</h2>
                    <div class="comparison-grid">
                        <div class="comparison-card">
                            <h4>Blazor WebAssembly</h4>
                            <p>Runs entirely in the browser using WebAssembly. No server connection required after initial load.</p>
                            <ul>
                                <li>✅ Offline capable</li>
                                <li>✅ No server resources needed</li>
                                <li>✅ Can be hosted as static files</li>
                                <li>❌ Larger download size</li>
                                <li>❌ Limited to browser capabilities</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                        <div class="comparison-card">
                            <h4>Blazor Server</h4>
                            <p>Runs on the server with UI updates sent over SignalR.</p>
                            <ul>
                                <li>✅ Fast initial load</li>
                                <li>✅ Full .NET runtime access</li>
                                <li>✅ Works on older browsers</li>
                                <li>❌ Requires constant connection</li>
                                <li>❌ Higher server load</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>How Blazor WebAssembly Works</h2>
                    <p>When a user visits your Blazor WebAssembly app:</p>
                    <ol>
                        <li>Browser downloads the HTML page</li>
                        <li>Downloads the Blazor JavaScript file</li>
                        <li>Downloads the .NET runtime (as WebAssembly)</li>
                        <li>Downloads your app's DLLs</li>
                        <li>.NET code runs directly in the browser</li>
                    </ol>
                    <pre><code class="language-html">&lt;!-- The magic starts here --&gt;
&lt;script src="_framework/blazor.webassembly.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>When to Use Blazor WebAssembly</h2>
                    <p>Blazor WebAssembly is ideal for:</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li>Progressive Web Apps (PWAs)</li>
                        <li>Apps that need offline support</li>
                        <li>Static site hosting (GitHub Pages, Azure Static Web Apps)</li>
                        <li>Single-page applications</li>
                        <li>When you want to share C# code with your backend</li>
                    </ul>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Your First Look at a Blazor Component</h2>
                    <pre><code class="language-razor">@@page "/counter"

&lt;h1&gt;Counter&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Current count: @@currentCount&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;button class="btn btn-primary" @@onclick="IncrementCount"&gt;
    Click me
&lt;/button&gt;

@@code {
    private int currentCount = 0;

    private void IncrementCount()
    {
        currentCount++;
    }
}</code></pre>
                    <p>This is a complete Blazor component! It combines HTML markup with C# code in a single <code>.razor</code> file.</p>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Key Takeaways</h2>
                    <div class="key-takeaways">
                        <div class="takeaway"><svg width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="20 6 9 17 4 12"/></svg><span>Blazor lets you build web UIs with C# instead of JavaScript.</span></div>
                        <div class="takeaway"><svg width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="20 6 9 17 4 12"/></svg><span>Blazor WebAssembly runs entirely in the browser.</span></div>
                        <div class="takeaway"><svg width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="20 6 9 17 4 12"/></svg><span>Components combine HTML and C# in .razor files.</span></div>
                        <div class="takeaway"><svg width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="20 6 9 17 4 12"/></svg><span>Choose WebAssembly for offline support and static hosting.</span></div>
                    </div>
                </section>
